  "notes": "Mid-2000s American teen-pop built on guitar plus programming: about 90 for the signature single and 100–120 for the uptempos, straight, backbeat on 2&4, sharp-side majors running I–V–vi–IV with a IV–V hold on the pre-chorus. The drum sentence supplies three deltas — \"kick 1 and 3\", \"8th hat with a 16th push before the backbeat\" (an 8th-weighted base with the fill density lifted so the push lands), and \"handclaps only in the chorus\" — plus the constraint that closes the block: \"no fills longer than a beat\", which moves `fills` out to 8 and 16 bars and drops the hat rolls to almost nothing. The bass rule is unusually specific and is encoded literally: roots with an occasional octave jump at the chorus, and **nothing below E1**, so the register floor sits at MIDI 28. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research says the tempo/key reading is **[LOW-CONF]** — an algorithmic catalogue value — and that the grammar is partly inferred. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities, the melody contour and the counter block; the entry gives \"melody syllabic and mid-register with the title as the highest note\" and \"backing vocals in unison-plus-3rd\", which is what the narrow register and the octave-echo counter are reading.",
